-
Continuous Execution
LLM chains analysis steps
@references
-
Report Generation
Citation for scientific writing

Critical Understanding:
- @description is the LLM's decision point - must clearly distinguish functions
- @usage is auto-generated - no need to write, LLM reads it from help files
- @param is NOT embedded - only read by LLM during code generation (keep concise: 2-3 lines each)
- @examples must be executable - LLM copies these patterns
- "What You Can Do Next" enables continuity - LLM knows how to proceed
  

---

📋 ENHANCED DOCUMENTATION STRUCTURE

Note on @usage: The @usage section is automatically generated by roxygen2 from the function definition. You do NOT need to write it manually. It will be available in the generated help files for LLM to read during code generation.


---

@title (准确+简短+有区分力)

Format: [Analysis Type] with [Method Name]

Good Examples:
#' Query Gene Perturbation Effects Across All Datasets
#' Find Upstream Regulators of Target Gene
#' Predict Therapeutic Targets from Disease Signature

Bad Examples:
#' Find Targets  ← Too vague
#' Query Database  ← Not function-specific


---

@description (2-3 sentences, 40-80 words)

🔍 Key Principle: Code-Logic-Driven Description

⚠️ CRITICAL: This is used for LLM function selection! 
- Embedded with @title as embedding1
- Returned to LLM for choosing the right function
- Must be clear, specific, and distinguishable from similar functions
  
Step 1: Read the code, understand what it ACTUALLY does
# Example: gpdb_find_targets.R
# Code shows:
# - Builds SQL query with filters
# - Queries gene_effects_agg table
# - Separates up/down regulated targets
# - Calculates statistics (consistency, confidence)
# - Returns structured list with 2 target groups

Step 2: Write description based on code logic

Sentence 1: What + How (Scientific Process + Data Source)
[Action Verb] [Specific Analysis Type] from [Database Reference] for [Use Case].

Example:
#' Identify downstream target genes regulated by perturbation, aggregated from 
#' 7,665 RNA-seq datasets for drug target discovery and pathway analysis.

Why this is better:
- ✅ "downstream target genes" - specific biological relationship
- ✅ "aggregated from 7,665 datasets" - clear data source
- ✅ "drug target discovery" - practical use case
- ❌ NOT "find targets" alone (too vague - what kind of targets?)
  
Sentence 2: When (Data Scope + Confidence)
**Database covers [scope]** with [confidence level] relationships.

Example:
#' **Database covers 2,810 genes across 1,063 cell lines** with 826,650 
#' high-confidence relationships (5+ datasets, >80% consistency).

Sentence 3: Why (OPTIONAL - Unique Output)

⚠️ ONLY include if method has UNIQUE output type

Test: Can other CCI methods produce the same output type?
- ✅ YES → DELETE Sentence 3
- ❌ NO → Include specific output
  
Example - ✅ Include (unique):
#' Returns confidence-scored relationships with multi-dataset consistency metrics.

Example - ❌ DELETE (not unique):
#' Identifies gene targets.  ← All perturbation analysis does this


---

@param (Clear & Concise for LLM Code Generation)

🔍 Key Principle: LLM needs to know what parameter does, valid values, and impact

Format (All Parameters):

#' @param param_name [Type]. [One-line summary]. 
#'   Default: \code{value}. Options/Range: [valid values]. 
#'   [One-line impact or usage note].

Example:

#' @param min_confidence Character. Evidence strength filter ("high", "medium", "low").
#'   Default: \code{"medium"}. Options: "high" (5+ datasets), "medium" (2-4), "low" (1). 
#'   Higher confidence gives reliable results; lower includes exploratory findings.

Key points:
- ✅ One line = one parameter (2-3 lines max)
- ✅ Include: Type, Default, Range/Options, Impact
- ✅ Biological rationale in one sentence
- ✅ No complex nested structures
  
Note: Parameters are NOT embedded. They are read by LLM during code generation. Keep them clear and concise so LLM can write correct function calls.


---

@references (Method Citation - MANDATORY)

🔍 Key Principle: If paper reference not found, search and add it

Format:

#' @references
#' [Authors] ([Year]). [Title]. [Journal] [Volume], [Pages]. \doi{[10.xxxx/xxxxx]}

**STANDARD GPSAdb DATABASE CITATION** (Use this for ALL GenePerturbR functions):

#' @references
#' Guo S, Xu Z, Dong X, Hu D, Jiang Y, Wang Q, Zhang J, Zhou Q, Liu S, Song W (2022).
#' GPSAdb: a comprehensive web resource for interactive exploration of genetic perturbation
#' RNA-seq datasets. Nucleic Acids Research, gkac1066. \doi{10.1093/nar/gkac1066}

**Database Statistics** (include in @description or @details when relevant):
- 7,665 RNA-seq datasets from genetic perturbation experiments
- 18.9M gene-gene relationships (826,650 high-confidence)
- 2,810 perturbed genes across 1,063 cell lines and 71 tissue types

Example for function-specific references:
#' @references
#' [Method-specific paper if applicable]
#' 
#' Guo S, Xu Z, Dong X, et al. (2022). GPSAdb: a comprehensive web resource for
#' interactive exploration of genetic perturbation RNA-seq datasets.
#' Nucleic Acids Research, gkac1066. \doi{10.1093/nar/gkac1066}

If reference not found in code:
1. Search function comments and dependencies
2. Search existing @references in other R files: grep -r "@references" R/*.R
3. If still not found → Use web_search: Search "[method/package name] paper citation DOI"
4. If web_search fails → Ask user: "Cannot find paper reference for [MethodName], please provide DOI"
5. ALWAYS include GPSAdb database citation (see standard format above)
  

---

@section Performance Test (MANDATORY - from real test)

#' @section Performance Test:
#' \itemize{
#'   \item **Test query**: [gene] ([query type])
#'   \item **Runtime**: [XX.XX sec] (database query + aggregation)
#'   \item **Result size**: [N] relationships returned
#'   \item **Database**: 18.9M relationships, optimized with SQLite indexing
#' }


---

@details (Scientific Context - OPTIONAL)

Only include if function output needs biological interpretation.

#' @details
#' **Interpreting Results**:
#' \itemize{
#'   \item **[Metric name]**: [What values mean biologically]
#'   \item **[Metric name]**: [How to interpret in biological context]
#' }

Example:
#' @details
#' **Interpreting Results**:
#' \itemize{
#'   \item **logfc_mean**: Average log2 fold change across datasets. |logFC| > 1.5 indicates strong effect.
#'   \item **consistency_score**: Fraction of datasets showing same direction (0.5-1.0). Score > 0.8 indicates reliable finding.
#'   \item **confidence**: Evidence classification based on n_datasets and consistency. "high" = 5+ datasets with >80% consistency.
#' }

Keep it minimal - Focus only on metrics that need biological context


---

@return (Clear Structure + Next Steps)

⚠️ CRITICAL: Use REAL test results from Phase 2!
- Run str(result, max.level = 2) to see actual structure
- Document actual class, elements, dimensions (not imagined)
- Use actual column names and data types from test output
  
Template:

#' @return [Class] object with [high-level description]
#'
#' **Return Structure**:
#' \describe{
#'   \item{\strong{result$element}}{[Biological meaning] ([Type], [Dimensions])}
#'     \itemize{
#'       \item Key columns: \code{column1} ([meaning]), \code{column2} ([meaning])
#'     }
#' }
#'
#' **What You Can Do Next**:
#' \enumerate{
#'   \item Filter significant results: \code{result$element[result$element$pvalue < 0.05, ]}
#'   \item Visualize: \code{\link{vis.MethodName}(result)}
#'   \item Downstream analysis: \code{\link{NextStepFunction}(result)}
#' }
#'
#' **Alternative Methods**:
#' \itemize{
#'   \item \code{\link{Alternative1}}: Use when [specific scenario]
#'   \item \code{\link{Alternative2}}: Use when [different scenario]
#' }


---

@examples (Reproducible + Scientifically Meaningful)

🔍 Key Principle: Examples Should Show What THIS Function Does, Not Entire Workflows

⚠️ CRITICAL: Use the SAME dataset and runtime from Phase 2 test!
- Dataset name, size, runtime must match actual test results
- Don't make up "Expected: 200-300 interactions" unless you ran the test
- Copy exact function call that was tested successfully
  
Note: Use \donttest{} instead of \dontrun{}:
- ✅ Users can run example(FunctionName) to test
- ✅ Examples are executable and verifiable
- ✅ Won't run during R CMD check (avoids timeout for long analyses)
- ✅ R manual compatible and follows CRAN best practices
  
Enhanced Format:

#' @examples
#' \donttest{
#' # ===========================================================================
#' # Example 1: Basic Usage (TESTED - runtime: X sec)
#' # ===========================================================================
#' # Scientific Question: [What this function answers]
#' # Query: [gene symbol] perturbation effects
#' # Expected output: [What to expect from THIS function]
#' 
#' # Zero configuration - database auto-loaded!
#' library(GenePerturbR)
#' 
#' result <- gpdb_find_targets(
#'   gene = "TP53",
#'   top_n = 100,
#'   min_confidence = "high"
#' )
#' 
#' # Verify output structure
#' str(result, max.level = 1)
#' cat("Targets found:", nrow(result$upregulated) + nrow(result$downregulated), "\n")
#' 
#' # Quick preview of results
#' head(result$upregulated)
#' head(result$downregulated)
#' 
#' # ===========================================================================
#' # Example 2: Key Parameter Variations (Show impact of 2-3 key params)
#' # ===========================================================================
#' # Compare how confidence levels affect results
#' 
#' # High confidence: strong evidence, fewer targets
#' result_high <- gpdb_find_targets(
#'   gene = "TP53",
#'   top_n = 50,
#'   min_confidence = "high"      # 5+ datasets, >80% consistency
#' )
#' 
#' # Medium confidence: include moderate evidence
#' result_medium <- gpdb_find_targets(
#'   gene = "TP53",
#'   top_n = 50,
#'   min_confidence = "medium"    # 2-4 datasets, >70% consistency
#' )
#' 
#' # Compare results
#' cat("High confidence:", nrow(result_high$upregulated), "targets\n")
#' cat("Medium confidence:", nrow(result_medium$upregulated), "targets\n")
#' 
#' # ===========================================================================
#' # Example 3: Effect Size Filtering (if applicable)
#' # ===========================================================================
#' # Focus on strong perturbation effects only
#' 
#' result_strong <- gpdb_find_targets(
#'   gene = "TP53",
#'   top_n = 50,
#'   min_confidence = "medium",
#'   min_effect_size = 1.5      # |logFC| > 1.5 only
#' )
#' 
#' cat("All effects:", nrow(result_medium$upregulated), "targets\n")
#' cat("Strong effects:", nrow(result_strong$upregulated), "targets\n")
#' cat("Average logFC (strong):", mean(abs(result_strong$upregulated$logfc_mean)), "\n")
#' 
#' # ===========================================================================
#' # Next Steps (Brief pointers to downstream analysis)
#' # ===========================================================================
#' # For complete workflows, see:
#' # - Enrichment analysis: ?gpdb_enrich
#' # - Visualization: ?gpdb_plot_network
#' # - Find regulators: ?gpdb_find_regulators
#' # - Compare genes: ?gpdb_compare_genes
#' # - Load raw data: ?gpdb_load_data
#' }

Why this is better:
- ✅ Focuses on what THIS function does (not 8-step workflows)
- ✅ Shows parameter impact through comparison
- ✅ Includes expected outputs for validation
- ✅ Brief pointers to downstream steps (not full implementation)
- ✅ Maintains scientific context without overextension
  

---

@seealso (Workflow-Oriented + Citation-Ready)

Enhanced Template:

#' @seealso
#' **Data Query** (explore database):
#' \itemize{
#'   \item \code{\link{gpdb_list_datasets}}: Find available datasets for a gene
#'   \item \code{\link{gpdb_get_info}}: Get detailed dataset metadata
#'   \item \code{\link{gpdb_load_data}}: Load raw expression data and DEG results
#' }
#'
#' **Downstream Analysis** (sequential use):
#' \itemize{
#'   \item \code{\link{gpdb_enrich}}: Pathway enrichment analysis (GO/KEGG/MSigDB)
#'   \item \code{\link{gpdb_plot_network}}: Visualize regulatory network
#'   \item \code{\link{gpdb_analyze_cascade}}: Trace multi-step regulatory paths
#'   \item \code{\link{gpdb_compare_genes}}: Compare perturbation effects across genes
#' }
#'
#' **Related Query Functions** (parallel comparison):
#' \itemize{
#'   \item \code{\link{gpdb_find_regulators}}: Find upstream genes that regulate target. 
#'     Use when: Want to know what controls gene X.
#'   \item \code{\link{gpdb_what_happens}}: Query all effects of gene perturbation. 
#'     Use when: Want comprehensive overview of gene knockout/knockdown.
#'   \item \code{\link{gpdb_predict_targets}}: Predict therapeutic targets from signature. 
#'     Use when: Have disease signature and need drug target candidates.
#'   \item \code{\link{gpdb_predict_interaction}}: Predict gene-gene interaction type. 
#'     Use when: Want to understand relationship between two genes.
#' }

Note: Original method citation and package info are in @references section.


---

@section User Queries (Task-Oriented + Data-Centric)

🔍 Purpose: These queries will be embedded separately (embedding2) for RAG retrieval. Focus on data analysis scenarios, not method details.

Query Patterns (Generate 10-20 queries):

#' @section User Queries:
#' \itemize{
#'   \item How to analyze [biological question] in my [tissue/cell type] data?
#'   \item My data shows [observation], which analysis should I use?
#'   \item Can I apply this to [species] [data type] dataset?
#'   \item How to compare [condition A] vs [condition B] in [tissue]?
#'   \item What to do if my [data type] has [specific characteristic]?
#'   \item Which parameters work best for [dataset size/quality]?
#'   \item How to interpret [result pattern] in [biological context]?
#'   \item My analysis returns [unexpected result], what does it mean?
#'   \item How to validate results in [experimental system]?
#'   \item What's the next step after finding [result type]?
#' }

Example for DoCCI.CellChat:
#' @section User Queries:
#' \itemize{
#'   \item What genes does TP53 regulate when knocked out?
#'   \item Which genes are most consistently affected by MYC perturbation?
#'   \item How do I find high-confidence targets for drug development?
#'   \item What happens to cell cycle genes when METTL3 is knocked down?
#'   \item Can I get targets specific to cancer cell lines?
#'   \item How reliable are the predicted gene-gene relationships?
#'   \item What tissue types have been studied for my gene of interest?
#'   \item How do I filter results by effect size and confidence?
#'   \item Which databases support my gene perturbation query?
#'   \item Can I compare perturbation effects between multiple genes?
#'   ... (10-20 total, focus on perturbation analysis scenarios)
#' }


---

🤖 AI AGENT EXECUTION WORKFLOW

TODO LIST Template (For Cursor Agent to Track Progress)

Before starting, create a TODO list using the following template:

## Documentation Optimization: [FunctionName]

### Phase 1: Code Understanding (CRITICAL)
- [ ] Read function source code (R/[FunctionName].R)
- [ ] Identify input → processing → output flow
- [ ] Understand database query logic (SQL queries, filters, aggregation)
- [ ] List all parameters and identify 2-3 key ones
- [ ] Check dependencies on other GenePerturbR functions

### Phase 2: Testing (CRITICAL - MUST PASS)
- [ ] Create test script with appropriate gene query
- [ ] Execute test successfully (database auto-configured)
- [ ] Record runtime (XX.XX sec for database query)
- [ ] Record result info (N relationships, confidence distribution)
- [ ] Document return structure (class, elements, data.frame columns)
- [ ] Save test results for @section Performance Test, @return, and @examples

### Phase 3: Documentation Writing (REQUIRED)
- [ ] @title: [Analysis Type] with [Method Name]
- [ ] @description: 2-3 sentences (What + How + When) - CRITICAL for LLM selection
- [ ] @param: All parameters (clear & concise, 2-3 lines each)
- [ ] @references: Paper + DOI (if not found, search and add)
- [ ] @section Performance Test: Runtime + Dataset info (from real test)
- [ ] @details: Interpreting Results (OPTIONAL - only if output needs biological context)
- [ ] @return: Return Structure + What You Can Do Next + Alternative Methods
- [ ] @examples: Example 1 - Basic Usage
- [ ] @examples: Example 2 - Key Parameter Variations
- [ ] @examples: Example 3 - Species/Special Usage (if applicable)
- [ ] @examples: Next Steps (brief pointers)
- [ ] @seealso: Upstream Preparation
- [ ] @seealso: Downstream Analysis
- [ ] @seealso: Alternative Methods (with use cases)
- [ ] @section User Queries: 10-20 task-centric analysis questions

### Phase 4: Validation (REQUIRED)
- [ ] No Chinese characters (grep check)
- [ ] No environment noise (≤1 sentence if needed)
- [ ] No filler words (robust/comprehensive/advanced)
- [ ] Has @references with Paper + DOI
- [ ] Has @section Performance Test with runtime from real test
- [ ] @description is 2-3 sentences
- [ ] @examples wrapped in \donttest{}
- [ ] @seealso links verified (functions exist)

### Phase 5: Final Review (RECOMMENDED)
- [ ] Test results match documented performance
- [ ] All code examples are syntactically correct
- [ ] Cross-references (\link{}) are valid
- [ ] Biological interpretation is scientifically accurate
- [ ] Documentation aligns with actual code logic

### Phase 6: Report Generation
- [ ] Generate Chinese summary report
- [ ] Include validation checklist results
- [ ] Provide formatted citation for Methods section

How to use this TODO list:
1. Copy the template at the start of documentation task
2. Check off items as you complete them
3. STOP if any CRITICAL item fails
4. Report progress to user with checklist status
  

---

Step-by-Step Protocol (What AI Should Do)

┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Phase 0: SETUP (Create TODO List)                          │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ 1. Copy TODO LIST template from above                      │
│ 2. Replace [FunctionName] with actual function name        │
│ 3. Keep TODO list visible throughout the process           │
│ 4. Check off items as you complete them                    │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
         ↓
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Phase 1: CODE UNDERSTANDING (Read Before Writing)          │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ 1. Read function source code (entire body)                 │
│ 2. Identify: Input type → Processing steps → Output type   │
│ 3. Find original paper (grep for DOI, citations, or URLs)  │
│    → If not found: use web_search for citation             │
│    → If web_search fails: ask user                         │
│ 4. List key parameters and their biological meaning        │
│ 5. Check for dependencies (other CellScope functions)      │
│ 6. ✓ Check off Phase 1 items in TODO list                  │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
         ↓
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Phase 2: TESTING (Verify Functionality)                    │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ 1. Create test script to run function with example query   │
│ 2. Execute test and measure runtime:                       │
│    ```                                                       │
│    Rscript -e "                                             │
│    library(GenePerturbR)  # Database auto-loads             │
│    start <- Sys.time()                                      │
│    result <- FunctionName(...)                             │
│    runtime <- as.numeric(difftime(Sys.time(), start,       │
│                          units='secs'))                     │
│    cat('Runtime:', runtime, 'sec\n')                        │
│    str(result, max.level = 2)                              │
│    "                                                         │
│    ```                                                       │
│ 3. If test fails → STOP and report error to user           │
│ 4. If test passes → Document:                              │
│    - Runtime (XX.XX sec) - measured from start to finish   │
│    - Result info (N relationships, confidence, genes)      │
│    - Return structure (class, elements, dimensions)        │
│ 5. Use these REAL results in @section Performance Test,    │
│    @return documentation, and @examples                     │
│    (not estimated or fabricated data)                       │
│ 6. ✓ Check off Phase 2 items in TODO list                  │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
         ↓
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Phase 3: DOCUMENTATION (Write Based on Code + Test)        │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ 1. @title: [Analysis] with [Method]                        │
│ 2. @description: 2-3 sentences (code-logic-driven)         │
│    - Sentence 1: What + How (include paper reference)      │
│    - Sentence 2: When (species + requirements)             │
│    - Sentence 3: Why (only if unique output)               │
│ 3. @param: All parameters (clear & concise)                │
│ 4. @references: Paper + DOI (search if not found)          │
│ 5. @section Performance Test (from test results)           │
│ 6. @details: Biological Interpretation (if needed)         │
│ 7. @return: Structure + What You Can Do Next + Alternatives│
│ 8. @examples: Basic usage + parameter comparison + pointers│
│ 9. @seealso: Upstream + Downstream + Alternative methods   │
│ 10. @section User Queries: 10-20 task-centric questions    │
│ 11. ✓ Check off Phase 3 items in TODO list as you write    │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
         ↓
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Phase 4: VALIDATION (Cursor Agent Direct Checks)           │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Cursor Agent should execute these checks directly:         │
│                                                              │
│ 1. Check for Chinese characters:                           │
│    grep -n "[\u4e00-\u9fff]" R/<function_file>.R           │
│                                                              │
│ 2. Check for forbidden environment keywords:               │
│    grep -i "first-time setup\|step-by-step.*install" \     │
│         R/<function_file>.R                                 │
│                                                              │
│ 3. Check for filler words:                                 │
│    grep -i "robust\|comprehensive\|advanced.*algorithm" \  │
│         R/<function_file>.R                                 │
│                                                              │
│ 4. Verify @references section exists:                      │
│    grep -A 3 "@references" R/<function_file>.R             │
│                                                              │
│ 5. Verify @section Performance Test exists (with real data):│
│    grep -A 3 "@section Performance Test" R/<function_file>.R│
│    # Should contain runtime and dataset from actual test   │
│                                                              │
│ 6. Check @description length (should be 2-3 sentences):    │
│    sed -n '/#'\'' @description/,/#'\''/p' R/<file>.R | \    │
│         wc -l  # Should be 3-5 lines                        │
│                                                              │
│ 7. Verify @examples has \donttest wrapper:                 │
│    grep -A 2 "@examples" R/<function_file>.R | \           │
│         grep "\\\\donttest"                                 │
│                                                              │
│ 8. Verify @seealso function links exist:                   │
│    # Extract linked functions and check if they exist      │
│    # Note: May not catch S3/S4 methods or imported funcs   │
│    grep "\\\\link{" R/<function_file>.R | \                │
│         sed 's/.*\\link{\([^}]*\)}.*/\1/' | while read f;do│
│           if grep -q "$f" R/*.R; then                      │
│             echo "✓ $f found in codebase"                  │
│           else                                              │
│             echo "⚠ Warning: $f not found (may be S3/S4)"  │
│           fi                                                │
│         done                                                │
│ 9. ✓ Check off Phase 4 validation items in TODO list       │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
         ↓
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Phase 5: REPORTING (中文输出 + TODO Progress)              │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ ✅ 文档优化完成：[FunctionName]                             │
│                                                              │
│ **代码理解**：[简述函数核心逻辑 - 输入/处理/输出]          │
│ **测试验证**：[dataset], [size] cells, [runtime] sec       │
│ **参考文献**：[Authors Year Journal DOI]                    │
│                                                              │
│ **关键修改**：                                              │
│ - @title: [Analysis Type] with [Method Name]               │
│ - @description: [2-3句话，基于代码逻辑]                     │
│ - @param: [n]个参数（简洁清晰，2-3行/参数）                │
│ - @references: 添加Paper + DOI（搜索并验证）               │
│ - @section Performance Test: Runtime + Dataset (真实测试)  │
│ - @details: 添加Biological Interpretation (if needed)      │
