🎨 VISUALIZATION FUNCTIONS - SPECIAL CONSIDERATIONS

Note: This section applies to visualization functions (e.g., gpdb_plot_* functions).

Unified Academic Theme (IMPORTANT)

All GenePerturbR visualization functions use a consistent academic publication theme.

The gpdb Theme System:
- Clean white background with minimal gridlines
- Publication-ready fonts and sizing
- Colorblind-friendly palettes (viridis, RdBu)
- Automatic dimension optimization for figures
- Consistent styling across all plot types
  

---

Standard Visualization Parameter Documentation

For visualization functions, document key parameters as:

#' @param colors Character vector. Custom color palette for the plot.
#'   Default: Automatically selected based on plot type (viridis for continuous, 
#'   RdBu for diverging). Provide vector of colors to override.
#' @param theme Character. ggplot2 theme to use: "academic" (default), "minimal", "classic".
#'   Academic theme optimized for publication with clean white background.

Example for enrichment dotplot:
#' @param color.by Character. Variable to map to point color: "p.adjust" (default), 
#'   "pvalue", "FoldEnrich". Controls color gradient in dotplot.


---

Standard Workflow for Visualization Functions

For gpdb_plot_* functions, follow this workflow:

Phase 1: Code Understanding
    ↓
Phase 1.5: THEME VERIFICATION (For gpdb_plot_* functions)
├─ Check current theme implementation
├─ Verify uses .gpdb_theme_default() or theme_bw()
├─ Check color palettes are publication-friendly
│   ├─ Continuous: viridis, plasma, inferno
│   ├─ Diverging: RdBu, BrBG (for fold changes)
│   └─ Categorical: ggplot2 defaults or custom palettes
└─ Test plot renders correctly
    ↓
Phase 2: Testing (verify visual output)
    ↓
Phase 3-5: Standard documentation workflow


---

Code Examples for Common Scenarios

Scenario 1: Volcano plot (up/down regulated genes)
# In function body:
p <- ggplot(data, aes(x = logFC, y = -log10(padj))) +
  geom_point(aes(color = regulation), size = 2) +
  scale_color_manual(values = c("up" = "red", "down" = "blue", "ns" = "grey"))

Scenario 2: Heatmap with diverging colors (fold changes)
# In function body:
scale_fill_gradientn(
  colors = c("#2166AC", "white", "#B2182B"),
  values = scales::rescale(c(-3, 0, 3)),
  limits = c(-3, 3)
)

Scenario 3: Enrichment dotplot (continuous p-values)
# In function body:
scale_color_viridis_c(option = "plasma", direction = -1)


